 {
  "id": "S150",
  "article_id": "nvblog:open-models-icml-2026",
  "title": "How Open Models Are Driving AI Research | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "At the International Conference on Machine Learning, nearly 145 papers cite NVIDIA Nemotron open models and datasets as the foundation for new research.\nEvery year, the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) reveals where thousands of AI researchers have decided to put their work.\nThis year’s accepted papers reveal a clear direction: open frontier models and open AI infrastructure have become foundational to how modern AI science gets done.\nNVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026",
  "passage": "…ts at a scale and speed that would’ve been impractical just a few years ago.\nThe Cosmos 3 family of open, frontier omnimodels gives researchers and developers a generational leap in the ability to build robots, autonomous vehicles and vision AI that perceive, reason, plan and act in the physical world.\nIn addition, the NVIDIA Alpamayo open model family for autonomous vehicle development, NVIDIA Isaac GR00T for robotics and NVIDIA BioNeMo for biomedical help accelerate research and development across industries.\nThe momentum extends beyond NVIDIA’s own research labs .\nBasecamp Research developed a new DNA foundation model, EDEN , that helps researchers interpret and design genetic sequences.\nMerck & Co. , uses KERMT to predict how potential drug molecules may behave in the body, including whether they are likely to be effective, safe and developable.\nSakana AI — attending ICML this year — built its Fugu and Fugu-Ultra models directly on Nemotron 3 Ultra, using the open foundation to push forward its work on AI research automation.\nKiloCode integrated Nemotron into its code-routing architecture, reporting token cost reductions of up to 90% — a result with real implications for the economics of deploying AI in production.\nNAVER developed its own model using the Nemotron architecture, extending the foundation for Korean-language AI research.\nTogether AI is hosting Nemotron models on its platform, making them more accessible to researchers who need reliable, seamless …",
  "statement": "Merck & Co. , uses KERMT to predict how potential drug molecules may behave in the body"
 },
 {
